[speech] lec04 (00:16:33.019 --> 00:18:03.120)
temporal and referential couplings. And you can have two variants of, you know, temporal couplings. So you can have systems that have components that are temporally coupled versus temporally decoupled, and then components that are referentially coupled and referentially decoupled. This creates now four categories of coordination in distributive systems. So let's start with the first one. So the direct coordination model refers to systems where the components are temporally coupled and referentially coupled. So it means that the components inside the distributive system, and when they communicate with each other, so temporally coupled means that they have to be up and running at the same time, and referentially coupled, you need that component initiates a request. It needs to know exactly what is the destination component. So as an example of the direct coordination model, we can use the cell phone system, right? So for example, if you want to be able to call someone, right, you need to know who you're calling. You cannot just call somebody in the ETA, right? You need to have a destination phone number that you're calling. So that's what it means by referentially coupled. You have to have a destination. And then temporary coupled means that both phones have to be up and running and connected to the network for the call to go through. If one of the phones is not connected to the network or is shut off, the call will not go through. You will get immediately an indication that, hey, you cannot call this number. All right.

[speech] lec04 (00:18:03.759 --> 00:19:49.680)
The mailbox coordination model refers to systems where the components are temporally decoupled but referentially coupled. So, temporally decoupled means that the components do not have to be up and running at the same time. the message will still be sent between them but you have to, again, referentially coupled means that a component needs to know with which other component does it need to talk to so as an example, I'm going to use electronic mail, email as a way to describe an example for this model so in the email model, the components represent the people that send emails between each other the system is temporally decoupled because I can send an email to one of my friends, even though they're not connected to their computer right now. The computer is offline. The email gets sent somewhere in the server where it gets stored. So we need some notion of persistency we'll see very shortly. But whenever I send an email, I cannot send the email to the ether. I need to send an email to a destination. The destination could be another email address, could be, for example, an email group. and the email group will then send an email to each of the members of the group but in the end we still need a destination. So this is an example of the mailbox coordination model. Now when we go to systems that are referentially decoupled we have two examples here. We have event-based systems and the shared data space model. So here, what they both have in common is that they have some sort of event bus where the components can actually submit the request directly to the event bus.

answer:
Yes—based on the lecture’s classification, **D2L notification system** is the best match.

On the slide, **temporally coupled + referentially decoupled** is the **event-based** model. The event-based diagram shows components publishing to an event bus and receiving **notification delivery**, which fits a notification system.

For the other options:
- **Linda tuple spaces / blog** → shared data space, temporally decoupled + referentially decoupled  
- **Electronic mail** → mailbox, temporally decoupled + referentially coupled  
- **Instant messaging** → direct, temporally coupled + referentially coupled
